# mente-status — show them it is on

🔴 **THE FAILURE THIS ANSWERS, measured 2026-09-07.** A full install succeeded
and the owner said: *"it is installed, it uses it now and then, but it has not
read how it must behave or where to begin."* ⛔ The commands existed as scripts
in `bin/`, so an assistant had to REMEMBER them — and a command nobody sees in
the `/` menu is a command that gets used now and then.

## Do this

```bash
Mente/bin/status
```

⭐ **Show its output and read it with them.** ⛔ Do not summarise it — the point
is that they see the system answer for itself.

Then, if they have not been told yet, hand over the whole interface **once**:

| They say | What happens |
|---|---|
| *"where were we?"* | I read where we left off — no guessing |
| *"what's still pending?"* | I read what was postponed |
| *"let's work on X"* | I open a piece of work and ask what I may touch |
| *"turn Mente OS off"* | ⛔ the checks pause · nothing is deleted |
| ⭐ *"let's close the session"* | **I write down where we got to, before this conversation resets** |

⚠️ **Say the last one out loud.** ⛔ It is the only one whose cost is invisible
until too late: the files survive a reset, the reasoning does not.

## ⛔ Never

- ⛔ Hand them a command to type. ⭐ They say the sentence, you run what it means.
- ⛔ Report green when a layer is down. ⚠️ If `bin/status` says a check is not
  wired, say so in their words — a tool whose job is telling you whether you are
  protected must never be the one that is wrong.

## ⬜ Where a path lives

⛔ **Do not hardcode a path from another installation.** ⭐ Every engine piece is
listed in `Mente/pieces.tsv` — read it when you need to know where something is,
and a moved piece stays findable without editing this file.
